# V. Risk factors

#1. VantageScore Competition (Maximum Risk)
This is not an assumed risk, but a reality that has already occurred. VantageScore is recognized by GSE, promoted by three credit bureaux at a price of 1/10, and the FIO monopoly in the mortgage market is being dismantled. If VantageScore gets a 30-50% share in the mortgage market within five years, the FICO rating business will face double pressure on revenue and pricing.

##2. Antimonopoly regulation
DOJ’s antimonopoly investigation may lead to FICO being asked to lower its rating pricing or being limited in its differentiated pricing strategy. Regulatory uncertainty itself suppresses valuation.

#3 Balance sheet risk
FIO’s shareholder interest is negative (due to large buybacks and debt-taking), meaning that financial flexibility is limited in the face of a negative business climate. Credit risk increases if the rating income falls significantly and the debt burden remains constant.

##4. Sustainability of the mark-up
FIO has been actively raising its rating prices in recent years (from $3-5 to $10), which is an important driver of income growth. But radical price increases are one of the reasons VantangeScore has gained market space. Continued price increases may accelerate client loss.

##5. Periodicity of the mortgage market
The value of the portfolio is highly correlated with the amount of mortgages issued.

